            <ArticleTitle>Second thoracic sympathetic ganglionectomy in sympathetically maintained pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>483-91</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Twenty-four individuals with sympathetically maintained pain were treated by posterior paravertebral T2 sympathectomy following transient response to sympathetic nerve blockade. Eight surgical patients (33.4%) had causalgia, and 16 patients (66.4%) suffered with reflex sympathetic dystrophy. Overall, physical evidence of improvement was noted in 87% of surgical patients, with subjective improvement in 71%. Reflex sympathetic dystrophy patients fared better than those with causalgia. Complications were minor. The techniques employed appear safe and effective; a multidisciplinary approach with neurosurgery, physiatry, anesthesiology, psychology, and allied health services is recommended.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
